                        "glosses": [
                            "Any extinct avialan in the family Hesperornithidae."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "1ccec862-1f8d-4e6c-8450-45e56f3a488a",
                                "sense_id": "8f414385-cf20-3050-9b05-c8b5a543f81a",
                                "text": "Finally, the Volgorod locality, Maastrichtian in age, has yielded two dinosaurs (an indeterminate hesperornithid and a basal theropod) and a few turtles."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "7c73ec66-2142-4ef9-9f73-f8158076d4e8",
                                "sense_id": "8f414385-cf20-3050-9b05-c8b5a543f81a",
                                "text": "The arrival of these hesperornithids in more southern waters during the early Campanian may indicate a cooler climate, closer shores, adaptations to changes in prey, or other factors."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d796c4c3-e9f3-4c7b-823d-f43a24f4ca19",
                                "sense_id": "8f414385-cf20-3050-9b05-c8b5a543f81a",
                                "text": "2020, Alan Feduccia, Romancing the Birds and Dinosaurs, Universal Publishers (BrownWalker Press), page 83,\n9. Cretaceous foot-propelled divers, the hesperornithids, were grouped cladistically with loons (Eocene) and grebes (Miocene) as a clade, which would require re-evolution of teeth from an edentulous ancestor."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
